    "localAnswers": [
      {
        "question": "Who led this",
        "title": "The Inter-Civic Council and C. K. Steele",
        "text": "Simulated · local helper\n\nAnswer from Mara\n\nThe Inter-Civic Council led the citywide Tallahassee bus boycott. Its elected president was Rev. C. K. Steele, pastor of Bethel Baptist Church. Students Wilhelmina Jakes and Carrie Patterson sparked the protest, and students and residents helped sustain it. The council called for seating on a first-come basis, courteous treatment and the hiring of Black drivers. Rev. Daniel B. Speed was among the other organizers who helped lead the campaign.\n\nQuoted from: The Inter-Civic Council and C. K. Steele.\n\nInspect answer record\nKeep this answer in my visit"
      },
      {
        "question": "Did it work?",
        "title": "From repeal to integrated buses",
        "text": "Simulated · local helper\n\nAnswer from Mara\n\nThe Tallahassee bus boycott helped end legally segregated seating on the city’s buses. On January 7, 1957, the city repealed its explicit segregation ordinance and gave drivers the power to assign seats. Riders who challenged that rule still faced arrest. Enforcement of assigned seating eased over the following months as Black riders returned. Florida Memory dates integration on Tallahassee’s buses to summer 1957.\n\nQuoted from: From repeal to integrated buses.\n\nInspect answer record\nKeep this answer in my visit"
      }
    ],
